@@ -0,0 +1,27 @@
+721. **Crew memory (K1, queue #455; the director, 14 Oct 2026, `docs/design/friendslop.md` §3, GDD App. F.6).**
+The friendslop audit's Friends goal (note 733: "It's extremely important that friends exceeds our target meaningfully") has a pillar, a crew remembers: in-jokes need a place to live, and a campaign the Wakers end should be a story to keep, not a reset.
+
+   - **What it was:**
+     - A night left nothing a crew kept together. The bookmark album (note 203) kept one machine's stills of the deaths; the profile (note 180) kept the commendations a player was given, by count.
+     - A campaign taken at dawn (note 632) was a closed save on the host's disk, and nothing on anyone else's.
+   - **Now** (memory.json, `CrewPhoto`, `CrewMemory`, `LogbookReader`, `Sim/Campaign/Logbook.cs`, `Hud.Memory.cs`, `GreyboxScene.Memory.cs`; GDD §9 Arrival, §11 "What a crew keeps", §3.1):
+     - **The crew photo at the gate** (`photo`): on a delivered night, `delaySeconds` (1.2) after this machine has the report, the app stages the shot (`CrewPhoto.Stage`) beside the engine on the yard's open side, away from the town's square (`CrewPhoto.YardSide`: the square's side has the terminus platform's canopy posts in front of any lens). The living stand in a line `out` (3 m) from the track, `spacing` (0.95 m) apart, ordered by id, each in the emote they're holding (caught `emoteAt` 0.8 s in) and sooty if sooted (note 717); bots stand in like anyone. The dead are their caps on a crate `crateGap` (1 m) past the line's right end, each in its crewmate's colours. The slate on an easel `slateOut` in front and `slateAside` left of the line has the tally chalked on it ("FRONTIER 7 / NIGHT 3 / NET 1450"). The lens is `cameraOut` (5.6 m) out, `cameraAhead` toward the engine's front, `fov` 46. The frame is drawn off screen like a bookmark's still, lit by a point light at the lens (`flashRange`, `flashStrength`), the screen flashes white over `flashSeconds`, and the bookmark cue clicks (`UiCue.Bookmark`). Who's in it comes only from the replicated world (`CrewPhoto.Sitters`: `CrewStates`, names, emotes, outfits, the soot flag), so every machine's photo is the same photo.
+     - **Kept**: `BookmarkAlbum.KeepPhoto` writes "00 crew photo.png" (`width` × `height`, 640 × 360) and a text sidecar (the slate, who stood left to right, whose caps) in the night's album folder. The end card shows it as a small framed print at the top left ("AT THE GATE").
+     - **U1's yard photographer** (docs/design/upgrades.md §4) is one call away: `CrewPhoto.Stage` on the departure's engine and `KeepPhoto(still, sidecar, name)` under a name of its own.
+     - **The logbook** (`logbook`): a ledger on a small shelf `height` (1.25 m) up the wall across the aisle from the kit locker in car one (`LogbookReader.Shelf`; the lockers' tops are up under the roof). `place` is the hook for the big cab's back wall (queue #450); every value but "lockers" is the lockers' for now. "THE LOGBOOK   READ : [E]" within `reach` (1.9 m) and `lookDegrees` (22°); Use opens it at the last night's page, the walking keys turn pages (and walk nobody while it's open), Use shuts it, dying shuts it. The press is kept from the host, as a word in town is. A page (`Hud.LogbookPage`, the route card's paper): whose log, NIGHT n, the line ("Frontier 7, Moss Settlement to Voss Crossing"), the date by the player's clock, how it ended, the photo pasted in if this machine has it (`PngWriter.Read` reads the album's PNG back), the incident report's cause-of-death lines and the train's own end, the commendations, the net.
+     - **Where it's kept**: the setup carries the campaign's crew name, its key (`Logbook.Key`: name and seed) and the night's number to every machine (`SessionSetup.Crew`, `CrewKey`, `Night`, left out of a quick night's JSON). Each machine writes the night's page into its own profile (`PlayerProfile.Data.Crews`, a `CrewLog` per crew) as the report comes in, again with the photo, and once more as the night's left with all its commendations (`Logbook.With` replaces a night's page in its place, keeping a photo). The host also puts it in the campaign's save (`CampaignState.Logbook`). A night that ends the campaign closes the crew in the profile.
+     - **The board of lost crews**: a fixture of its own (`lostcrews`, the notice board's size) in every town's square, `LostCrewsAlong` (10.6 m) along from the square's middle beside the notice board, appended last so every other fixture keeps its index. Its cards are the player's own ended crews (`Logbook.Lost`, `Logbook.Card`: "The Night Mail" / "Ran 5 nights. TAKEN AT DAWN. PRIYA: Taken at dawn by a Waker on the line at km 21. ..."), drawn on its face as the notices are (`WorldArt.LostCrews`, a heading card and up to three) and read close up a card at a time ("THE BOARD OF LOST CREWS   READ : [E]", `TownTalk.LostCrews`). With none posted it reads its own words.
+   - **No protocol change**: the report already travels whole as JSON; the crew, key and night are setup fields an older peer reads past (as `CampaignNight` is).
+   - **Checked:**
+     - `CrewMemoryTests` (Sim, 5): a page keeps the report's death and derailment lines (not the grabs or rescues), the commendations and the net; a night written again replaces its own page and keeps its photo; the campaign's book round-trips the save and outlives the campaign, an old save reads empty; an ended campaign is on the board with its card, an open one isn't; every town's square has the board beside the notice board, clear of it, targeted from in front.
+     - `CrewMemoryTests` (Game, 7): the photo's sitters by id, living first, emote and soot from the world; the line out from the engine a shoulder apart, left to right in the lens, the crate past its right end, the slate left, everyone facing the lens and in frame, the same from a reshuffled crew; the photo and its sidecar kept and read back; every machine's profile keeps the page through to the board after a campaign taken at dawn, and the setup carries the crew; the save slots keep the book; the shelf's prompt and the reader's pages; the page's layout stays on its sheet at 480 × 270 and 320 × 180; the board read a card at a time.
+     - Views `crewphoto` and `lostcrews` (gallery), and `dt screenshot --hud --report brought --photo out/shots/crewphoto.png` (the end card's print), `--hud --logbook 3 --photo ...` (the page), `--hud --talk lostcrews --again 1` (a card read), `--hud --shelf` (the shelf's prompt) rendered and looked at.
+   - **Left for later:**
+     - The book on the big cab's back wall (queue #450's photo rail and gimmicks): set `place` and give `LogbookReader.Shelf` the wall's spot.
+     - A shutter sound of its own (AU1's): the bookmark's click stands in.
+     - Opening the logbook between nights (the fortress's menus), and the photo in a headset's panel.
+     - The soot (note 717) reads faintly at the photo's distance, over the masks: a stronger mark for the photo if the director wants it.
+
+**Handoff** (14 Oct, K1)
+- Done: built on K1's branch, for K1 to integrate.
+- Next: the cab's back wall takes the book (#450); U1's departure photo reuses `CrewPhoto.Stage` and `BookmarkAlbum.KeepPhoto`.
